---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';

const title = 'Privacy — Your Files Never Leave Your Browser | NoWatermark';
const description =
  'How NoWatermark handles your data: files are processed locally and never uploaded, there is no account, no storage and no file-derived analytics.';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonicalPath="/privacy">
  <div class="nw-shell-narrow pt-8">
    <Breadcrumbs trail={[{ href: '/privacy', label: 'Privacy' }]} />

    <header class="nw-panel nw-calm-header mt-6">
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold">Privacy</h1>
      <p class="mt-3 text-lg nw-muted">
        The short version: your files never leave your browser, and we could not read them if we
        wanted to.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="nw-content mt-10">
      <h2>Files you scan or clean</h2>
      <p>
        Files you put into our tools are processed entirely on your own device. There is no upload
        endpoint on this site. There is no server that receives files, no database, and no object
        storage. We do not have a copy of your file, and we never did.
      </p>
      <p>Specifically, we never transmit or store:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>your file's contents, in whole or in part</li>
        <li>hashes or fingerprints derived from your file</li>
        <li>your filenames</li>
        <li>metadata values extracted from your file, including GPS, timestamps and author fields</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        You can confirm this yourself: load a tool page, disconnect from the internet, and scan or
        clean a file. Everything works. Or watch your browser's network panel while you scan —
        nothing carrying your file appears.
      </p>

      <h2>Text you paste — and the one thing that is sent</h2>
      <p>
        Checking pasted text for hidden characters, and cleaning them out, happens entirely on your
        device like everything else. Nothing is sent.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>There is one exception, and it is the only one on this site.</strong> The text
        scanner offers a <em>rewrite</em> option, which rewords your text in an attempt to disturb a
        statistical AI text watermark. A statistical watermark is encoded in word choice, so the
        only way to disturb it is to change the words — and that needs a language model, which we
        cannot run inside your browser.
      </p>
      <p>
        If, and only if, you use that option, the text in the box is sent to
        <strong>Google's Gemini API</strong> through a proxy on this site, and the rewritten text is
        sent back. This happens per use: you press the button, you are shown the exact request body
        first, and nothing is remembered between uses. There is no setting that turns it on
        permanently, by design.
      </p>
      <p>What is sent is only the text you typed or pasted. Never a file, a filename or a hash.</p>
      <p>
        We do not log the request body and we store nothing. But once the text reaches Google it is
        governed by Google's terms, not by this page, and you should know what those say before you
        use it.
      </p>
      <p>
        This feature uses the <strong>paid tier</strong> of the Gemini API, which matters, because
        the two tiers are governed by different terms. On the paid tier Google states that it does
        <strong>not</strong> use prompts or responses to improve its products, and does not use them
        to train its models. On the unpaid tier it does both, and human reviewers may read the
        content. We pay for this specifically so the second set of terms does not apply to your
        text.
      </p>
      <p>
        Google does still log prompts and responses for a limited period — by default up to 55 days
        — solely to detect abuse of their service and to meet legal obligations. After that the logs
        are deleted. So the text is not retained indefinitely, but it is not instantaneous either,
        and during that window it exists on Google's systems rather than only on your device.
      </p>
      <p>
        We are being precise about this rather than saying "Google doesn't keep it", because that
        would not be true. What is true: it is not used to train anything, it is not read for
        product improvement, and it is deleted.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Even so, this is the one feature on the site where your content leaves your
        machine.</strong> If a piece of text is genuinely sensitive, the honest advice is not to
        send it — use the local hidden-character cleaner, which never transmits anything, and accept
        that statistical watermarks are beyond what any on-device tool can address.
      </p>
      <p>
        Google's terms are at
        <a href="https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/terms" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank"
          >ai.google.dev/gemini-api/terms</a
        >. If the tier or the terms change, this page changes in the same release.
      </p>
      <p>
        We cannot tell you whether a rewrite worked. There is no watermark detector we can run, so
        the result is labelled <strong>"Rewritten — unverified"</strong> and is never counted as a
        removal anywhere in the interface.
      </p>

      <h2>Accounts</h2>
      <p>
        There are none. Every tool works without registration, and we hold no user records because
        there are no users to record.
      </p>

      <h2>Analytics</h2>
      <p>We use two analytics tools, and they behave differently.</p>
      <p>
        <strong>Cloudflare Web Analytics</strong> is cookieless. It does not fingerprint visitors
        and does not track people across sites. It records page URLs, referrers, general performance
        timings and coarse location at country level.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Google Analytics 4</strong> is not cookieless. It sets cookies in your browser
        (<code>_ga</code> and related), assigns your browser a pseudonymous identifier so returning
        visits can be recognised, and sends that data to Google, who may use it in line with their
        own policies. We use it to understand which pages and search terms bring people here.
      </p>
      <p>
        Neither tool ever receives anything derived from your file or your pasted text. That is a
        hard architectural boundary, not a setting: files are read and cleaned entirely on your
        device and are never transmitted, so there is nothing about their contents for an analytics
        tool to collect. The rewrite option described above sends text to Google's Gemini API only,
        never to an analytics tool, and it sends no event recording that you used it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Analytics is optional to the product. If you block it — with an extension, a tracker
        blocker, or Do Not Track — every tool continues to work exactly the same.
      </p>

      <h2>Cookies</h2>
      <p>
        Google Analytics sets cookies, as described above. Cloudflare Web Analytics does not, and we
        set none of our own for logins, preferences or tracking, because there is nothing to log in
        to and nothing stored.
      </p>
      <p>
        If we introduce advertising in future, ad providers may set their own cookies, and this page
        will be updated to say so plainly before that happens.
      </p>

      <h2>Advertising</h2>
      <p>
        This site is not currently running advertising. If it does in future, ad slots will sit in
        clearly separated areas, never adjacent to download controls or styled to resemble product
        buttons — and advertising will never require your file to be uploaded, because uploading is
        not a thing this site does.
      </p>

      <h2>Hosting</h2>
      <p>
        The site is served as static files by Cloudflare Pages. Cloudflare processes standard
        request information such as IP address and user agent in order to deliver the page and
        protect against abuse, as any web host does. That is request-level data about visiting a
        page, unrelated to the files you process.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        If this policy changes materially — particularly anything touching the local-processing
        guarantee — we will say so on this page rather than quietly editing it.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about privacy can go to <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="h-16"></div>
</BaseLayout>
